          "title": "You'll want to know: der Zoll",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "der Zoll — \"the customs; the duty (tax)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Beim Zoll fragt ein Beamter nach Zigaretten. At customs an officer asks about cigarettes."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Plural: die Zölle, for the taxes. Der Zoll is also an inch, a different word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Zoll comes from Old High German zol (EtymDB), and it is usually traced to Late Latin tolōneum 'toll house', from Greek telṓnion. EtymDB instead links it with a Germanic word for what is counted, so the line is hedged. English toll is a cousin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
